From 1d85d5c82cd77edd91b9b922b2d63f3576650f93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrin Jalali <adrin.jalali@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 16:53:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] gh-152235: Track sets filled from an iterator ahead of
 the new elements

Since gh-152235, set(iterable), frozenset(iterable), set.union(),
set.intersection() and set.symmetric_difference() only GC-track the new
set once it is full, so that it can't be reached half-built.  When the
iterable creates the elements, as a generator does, the set then sits
behind them in the GC list: a full collection reaches the elements first,
parks them as tentatively unreachable and, once it reaches the set, moves
them back in hash order.  That scatters them in memory, and every later
full collection is up to ten times slower.

Add an internal GC API to track an object at a position reserved before
it was filled, _PyGC_ReservePosition() and _PyObject_GC_TrackAtPosition(),
and use it in those set constructors.  The iterator being consumed lends
its position when that is safe; otherwise a placeholder is tracked.  The
half-built set is still not reachable through the GC.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
